  "translation": {
    "literal": "At the Qingming season the rain falls in a fine drizzle; travellers on the road are ready to lose their souls. May I ask where there is a tavern? A cowherd boy points far off toward Apricot Blossom Village.",
    "poetic": "It is Qingming, and the rain keeps falling; the travellers on the road feel their hearts break. \"Is there an inn anywhere near?\" I ask, and a cowherd points far away to Apricot Blossom Village."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "festival",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Qingming",
      "body": "清明 falls in early April and is the day families sweep the graves of their ancestors. A traveller away from home on that day has a double reason for sorrow: 斷魂 means grief that \"cuts off the soul\", not a literal death."
    },
    {
      "id": "author",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Is it really Du Mu's?",
      "body": "The poem is traditionally credited to Du Mu (803–852), but it is not found in his own collected works. It appears first in a Song-era anthology, 千家詩, so some scholars doubt the attribution. It remains one of the most loved short poems in the language. Several places, in Anhui and Shanxi, claim to be the real 杏花村."
    },
    {
      "id": "shape",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "A poem that is all question and answer",
      "body": "The first two lines set the mood and the last two are a tiny story with a question and a reply. We never hear the reply, only see a pointing finger. The poem ends on a patch of apricot blossom, the first hint of colour and warmth against the grey rain."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 紛 · 魂 · 村",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 紛 (fēn), 魂 (hún) and 村 (cūn). They rhyme in modern speech (-n). In the old rhyme books 紛 belongs to a neighbouring group, and a jueju may use neighbouring rhymes."
    }
  ],
